<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:14.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold'>OSI Model Protocols:</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0=C2=A0=C2=A0=C2=A0=C2=A0=C2=A0=C2=A0=C2=A0=
 </span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri'><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold;font-size:15.0pt;color:#00B050'>Port numbers prim=
arily
belong to the Transport Layer (Layer 4).</span><span style=3D'font-size:11.=
0pt'>
They allow the Transport Layer protocols (TCP and UDP) to direct data to the
correct application or service running on a device.</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>Here's
a simple, layer-wise explanation with common protocols and their typical po=
rt
numbers, keeping in mind that<span style=3D'font-weight:bold'> port numbers=
 are
explicitly used at Layer 4</span> and above for application identification:=
<br
/>
&nbsp;</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt;
color:#C00000'><span style=3D'font-weight:bold'>7. Application Layer (Close=
st to
the user)</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold'>* Function:</span><span style=3D'font-weight:bol=
d;
color:#0070C0'> Provides network services directly to end-user applications.
This is where users interact with network applications.</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold'>* Protocols</span><span style=3D'font-weight:bol=
d;
color:#C00000'> (and their common default ports, which they use via TCP/UDP=
):</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;*
HTTP: (Hypertext Transfer Protocol) - Web Browse. TCP Port 80</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;*
HTTPS: (Hypertext Transfer Protocol Secure) - Secure web Browse. TCP Port 4=
43</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;*
<span style=3D'font-weight:bold;color:#7030A0'>FTP:</span> (File Transfer
Protocol) - File transfer.<span style=3D'font-weight:bold;color:#C00000'> T=
CP
Ports 20 (data) &amp; 21 (control)</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;*
SSH: (Secure Shell) - Secure remote access. TCP Port 22</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;*
<span style=3D'font-weight:bold;color:#7030A0'>Telnet:</span> (Teletype Net=
work)
- Unencrypted remote access. TCP <span style=3D'font-weight:bold;color:#C00=
000'>Port
23</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;*
<span style=3D'font-weight:bold;color:#7030A0'>SMTP:</span> (Simple Mail Tr=
ansfer
Protocol) - Sending email. TCP <span style=3D'font-weight:bold;color:#C0000=
0'>Port
25</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;*
DNS: (Domain Name System) - Translates domain names to IP addresses. Primar=
ily
UDP Port 53 (for queries), but also TCP Port 53 (for zone transfers).</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri'><span
style=3D'font-size:11.0pt'>&nbsp;&nbsp;</span><span style=3D'font-weight:bo=
ld;
font-size:11.0pt'>&nbsp;* </span><span style=3D'font-weight:bold;font-size:=
13.0pt;
color:#00B050'>DHCP: (Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol) - Assigns IP
addresses. </span><span style=3D'font-weight:bold;font-size:11.0pt;color:#C=
00000'>UDP
Ports 67 (server) &amp; 68 (client)</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;*
<span style=3D'font-weight:bold;color:#7030A0'>POP3</span>: (Post Office Pr=
otocol
version 3) - Retrieving email. TCP <span style=3D'font-weight:bold;color:#C=
00000'>Port
110</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;*<span
style=3D'font-weight:bold;color:#7030A0'> IMAP</span>: (Internet Message Ac=
cess
Protocol) - Retrieving and managing email. <span style=3D'font-weight:bold;
color:#C00000'>TCP Port 143</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;*
<span style=3D'font-weight:bold;color:#7030A0'>NTP:</span> (Network Time
Protocol) - Synchronizing clocks.<span style=3D'font-weight:bold;color:#C00=
000'>
UDP Port 123</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;*
RDP: (Remote Desktop Protocol) - Remote desktop access. TCP Port 3389<br />
&nbsp;</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt;
color:#C00000'><span style=3D'font-weight:bold'>6. Presentation Layer</span=
></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold'>* Function: </span><span style=3D'font-weight:bo=
ld;
color:#0070C0'>Handles data formatting, encryption, decryption, and
compression. It ensures data is presented in a readable format for the
Application Layer.</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold'>* Protocols: </span><span style=3D'font-weight:b=
old;
color:#C00000'>No specific public &quot;port numbers&quot; associated direc=
tly
with this layer's protocols</span>, as they typically operate within the da=
ta
stream managed by Application Layer protocols. Examples include:</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;*
JPEG, MPEG, GIF: (Image/Video formats) - Deal with data representation.</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;*
ASCII, EBCDIC: (Character encoding)</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;*
SSL/TLS: (Secure Sockets Layer / Transport Layer Security) - Often works ac=
ross
this and the Session/Transport layers for encryption/decryption, encapsulat=
ing
application data.<br />
&nbsp;</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt;
color:#C00000'><span style=3D'font-weight:bold'>5. Session Layer</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold'>* Function: </span><span style=3D'font-weight:bo=
ld;
color:#0070C0'>Establishes, manages, and terminates communication sessions
between applications. It controls dialogues.</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold'>* Protocols:</span> Similar to the Presentation =
Layer,
<span style=3D'font-weight:bold;color:#C00000'>no specific public &quot;port
numbers&quot; for these protocols</span> themselves. They manage the session
using services provided by the layers below. Examples include:</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;*
NetBIOS: (Network Basic Input/Output System)</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;*
RPC: (Remote Procedure Call)</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;*
PPTP: (Point-to-Point Tunneling Protocol) - Used for VPNs.<br />
&nbsp;</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt;
color:#C00000'><span style=3D'font-weight:bold'>4. Transport Layer</span></=
p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold'>* Function: </span><span style=3D'font-weight:bo=
ld;
color:#0070C0'>This is the layer where port numbers are defined and used. It
provides reliable (or unreliable) end-to-end data delivery between specific
applications using these port numbers for &quot;service-point addressing.&q=
uot;</span><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold;color:#C00000'> It handles segmentation, reassemb=
ly,
flow control, and error control.</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold'>* Protocols</span> (which use port numbers):</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;*
TCP: (Transmission Control Protocol) - Connection-oriented, reliable.</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;*
UDP: (User Datagram Protocol) - Connectionless, unreliable.</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;*
(Note: The specific port numbers like 80, 443, 21, etc., are used by TCP or=
 UDP
to identify the application running on that port.)<br />
&nbsp;</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt;
color:#C00000'><span style=3D'font-weight:bold'>3. Network Layer</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold'>* Function: </span><span style=3D'font-weight:bo=
ld;
color:#0070C0'>Handles</span><span style=3D'font-weight:bold;color:#C00000'>
logical addressing (IP addresses)</span><span style=3D'font-weight:bold;
color:#0070C0'> and </span><span style=3D'font-weight:bold;color:#C00000'>r=
outing
of data packets</span><span style=3D'font-weight:bold;color:#0070C0'> across
different networks. It determines</span><span style=3D'font-weight:bold;
color:#C00000'> the best path for data.</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold'>* Protocols: </span><span style=3D'font-weight:b=
old;
color:#C00000'>No concept of &quot;port numbers&quot;</span> at this layer.
Instead,<span style=3D'font-weight:bold;color:#C00000'> it uses IP addresse=
s</span>
for device identification.</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;*
IP: (Internet Protocol) - The core protocol for routing packets (IPv4, IPv6=
).</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;*
ICMP: (Internet Control Message Protocol) - Used for network diagnostics (e=
.g.,
ping).</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;*
ARP: (Address Resolution Protocol) - Maps IP addresses to MAC addresses
(operates between Layer 2 and 3).</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;*
Routing Protocols (e.g., OSPF, BGP): Determine paths.<br />
&nbsp;</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt;
color:#C00000'><span style=3D'font-weight:bold'>2. Data Link Layer</span></=
p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold'>* Function: </span><span style=3D'font-weight:bo=
ld;
color:#0070C0'>Provides node-to-node data transfer within a single network
segment. It handles physical addressing (MAC addresses).</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold'>* Protocols: </span><span style=3D'color:#C00000=
'>No
concept of &quot;port numbers.</span>&quot; Instead, it <span style=3D'font=
-weight:
bold;color:#C00000'>uses MAC addresses for local device identification.</sp=
an></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;*
Ethernet: Most common wired LAN technology.</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;*
Wi-Fi (IEEE 802.11): Wireless LAN technology.</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;*
PPP: (Point-to-Point Protocol)<br />
&nbsp;</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt;
color:#C00000'><span style=3D'font-weight:bold'>1. Physical Layer (Closest =
to the
physical medium)</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold'>* Function: </span><span style=3D'font-weight:bo=
ld;
color:#0070C0'>Deals with the physical transmission of raw bit streams over=
 a
physical medium (cables, radio waves).</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold'>* Protocols/Standards:</span><span style=3D'font=
-weight:
bold;color:#C00000'> No concept of &quot;port numbers&quot;</span> or logic=
al
addresses.<span style=3D'font-weight:bold;color:#C00000'> Deals with voltage
levels, cable types, connectors, radio frequencies, etc.</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;*
Cables: (Ethernet cables, fiber optics)</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;*
Connectors: (RJ-45)</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;*
Wireless signals: (Radio frequencies)<br />
&nbsp;</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>Key
Takeaway: Port numbers are an essential part of the Transport Layer (Layer =
4),
allowing the protocols at that layer (TCP and UDP) to identify which
Application Layer (Layer 7) service or process on a specific device should
receive the incoming data.</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><br
/>
&nbsp;</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:14.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold'>Transmission Control Protocol (TCP):<br />
</span>&nbsp;</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:-apple-system;font-si=
ze:
10.5pt'><span style=3D'font-weight:bold'>Transmission Control Protocol (TCP=
)</span>
is a core Internet protocol that provides <span style=3D'font-weight:bold;
color:#C00000'>reliable, ordered, and error-checked delivery of data stream=
s</span><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold;color:#0070C0'> between applications communicatin=
g over
an IP network, ensuring data arrives completely and in the correct sequence=
.</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:-apple-system;font-si=
ze:
10.5pt'>&nbsp;</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:-apple-system;font-si=
ze:
10.5pt'><span style=3D'font-weight:bold'>User Datagram Protocol (UDP)</span=
> is a
<span style=3D'font-weight:bold;color:#C00000'>connectionless transport lay=
er
protocol</span> that provides <span style=3D'font-weight:bold;color:#C00000=
'>a
fast, but unreliable, &quot;best-effort&quot; delivery of data packets</spa=
n><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold;color:#0070C0'> without establishing a prior conn=
ection
or guaranteeing delivery order.</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:-apple-system;font-si=
ze:
10.5pt'>&nbsp;</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:-apple-system;font-si=
ze:
14.0pt'><span style=3D'font-weight:bold'>Most of the protocols use TCP and =
UDP?</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:-apple-system;font-si=
ze:
10.5pt'>It's<span style=3D'font-weight:bold'> accurate to say that most wid=
ely
used </span><span style=3D'font-weight:bold;color:#C00000'>application-layer
protocols rely on TCP rather than UDP for their transport.<br />
<br />
</span>&nbsp;</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in'><img
src=3D"OSIModel,TCPIPModelandProtocols_files/image011.jpg" width=3D522 heig=
ht=3D285
alt=3D"Common TCP-IP Port Numbers.."></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in'><span style=3D'font-family:-appl=
e-system;
font-size:10.5pt'>&nbsp;</span><span style=3D'font-weight:bold;font-family:=
Calibri;
font-size:14.0pt'><br />
<br />
</span><span style=3D'font-weight:bold;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.0pt;
color:#0070C0'>TCP Segment Structure:</span><span style=3D'font-weight:bold;
font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.0pt;color:#B43512'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0 </span>A single TCP segment can carry bot=
h outgoing
data AND an acknowledgment for incoming data. This &quot;piggybacking&quot;=
 of
ACKs within data segments is a common optimization in TCP.<br />
<br />
</span><span style=3D'font-weight:bold;font-family:Calibri;font-size:13.0pt;
color:#00B050'>Piggybacking --&gt;</span><span style=3D'font-family:Calibri;
font-size:13.0pt;color:#B43512'><span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0 </s=
pan></span><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold;font-family:Calibri;font-size:15.0pt'>A technique=
 </span><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold;font-family:Calibri;font-size:13.0pt;color:#7030A=
0'>where
an acknowledgment (ACK) for received data is attached to an outgoing data
packet</span><span style=3D'font-family:Calibri;font-size:13.0pt'>, rather =
than
sending a separate, dedicated ACK packet.<br />
<br />
</span><span style=3D'font-weight:bold;font-family:Calibri;font-size:13.0pt;
color:#00B050'>Sliding Window Technique:</span><span style=3D'font-family:C=
alibri;
font-size:13.0pt'><br />
The sliding window technique is a flow control method </span><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold;font-family:Calibri;font-size:13.0pt;color:#7030A=
0'>that
