Flow:</span><span style=3D'font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.0pt'>Imagine a =
letter
being sent through a mail system.</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri'><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold;font-size:15.0pt'>TTL (Time To Live)</span><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold;font-size:11.0pt'> is like a </span><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold;font-size:15.0pt;background:yellow;mso-highlight:=
yellow'>&quot;hop
limit&quot;</span><span style=3D'font-weight:bold;font-size:11.0pt'> or </s=
pan><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold;font-size:15.0pt;background:yellow;mso-highlight:=
yellow'>&quot;maximum
journey count</span><span style=3D'font-weight:bold;font-size:11.0pt'>&quot;
written on that letter.</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>Every
time the letter passes through a new sorting office (a router on the intern=
et),
this number is reduced by one.</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold'>If the number reaches zero before the letter get=
s to
its destination, the sorting office throws the letter away.</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>This
prevents letters <span style=3D'font-weight:bold'>from endlessly circulatin=
g the
mail system if there's a mistake in the address or routing</span>, which wo=
uld
otherwise clog up the entire system.<br />
<br />
<br />
&nbsp;</p>

