</span>&nbsp;</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>&nbsp;</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:14.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold'>TCP's reliability during connection termination:=
 what
happens if the final ACK is lost?</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>Let's
clarify the scenario and the outcome:</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:14.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold'>1. If the final ACK from client to server reache=
s the
server:</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>*
Client's Action: The client, after sending the final ACK, immediately enters
the TIME_WAIT state. It waits for 2 * MSL (Maximum Segment Lifetime).</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>*
Server's Action: Upon receiving the final ACK, the server (which was in the
LAST_ACK state) transitions directly to the CLOSED state. Its resources for
that connection are fully released.</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>*
Connection Status: Both sides consider the connection fully closed after the
TIME_WAIT period on the client side expires.</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>&nbsp;</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:14.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold'>2. If the final ACK from client to server is los=
t:</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>This
is where TCP's robust error handling comes into play.</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>*
<span style=3D'font-weight:bold'>Client's Action:</span> The <span
style=3D'font-weight:bold'>client has sent the final ACK and immediately en=
tered
TIME_WAIT.</span> It is now <span style=3D'font-weight:bold'>silently waiti=
ng for
2 * MSL.</span> It <span style=3D'font-weight:bold'>does not know if its AC=
K was
lost initially.</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>*<span
style=3D'font-weight:bold'> Server's Action:</span> The server, in the LAST=
_ACK
state, is<span style=3D'font-weight:bold'> expecting that final ACK from the
client side</span>. Since<span style=3D'font-weight:bold'> it doesn't recei=
ve it
within a certain timeout period</span>, <br />
<span style=3D'font-weight:bold'>it assumes its FIN segment</span> (the one=
 it
sent in step 3 of the four-way handshake) <span style=3D'font-weight:bold'>=
was
not acknowledged.</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold'>* Server Retransmits FIN:</span> Because<span
style=3D'font-weight:bold;color:#0070C0'> TCP guarantees reliable delivery<=
/span>,
<span style=3D'font-weight:bold'>the server will retransmit its FIN segment=
 to
the client.</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold'>* Client in TIME_WAIT Receives Retransmitted FIN=
:</span>
When the client, which is in the TIME_WAIT state, receives this retransmitt=
ed
FIN, it knows what to do: it will re-send its final ACK. It does not initia=
te a
new connection or try to send data. It simply ensures the other side gets t=
he
acknowledgment for its FIN.</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold'>* Server Receives Retransmitted ACK:</span> The =
server
eventually receives this retransmitted ACK, confirms that its FIN has been
acknowledged, and then transitions to the CLOSED state.</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold'>* Client's TIME_WAIT Continues:</span> The clien=
t's
TIME_WAIT timer resets or continues, ensuring the 2 * MSL duration is met,
after which it also fully closes.</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>&nbsp;</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>Key
Takeaway: <span style=3D'font-weight:bold;color:#0070C0'>The TIME_WAIT stat=
e is
precisely designed to handle the loss of the final ACK.</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>Without
TIME_WAIT, if the final ACK was lost and the client immediately closed, the
server would be stuck in LAST_ACK retransmitting its FIN indefinitely (or u=
ntil
its retransmission timers expired and it forcibly reset the connection, whi=
ch
is not a graceful shutdown). <span style=3D'font-weight:bold;color:#B43512'=
>The
TIME_WAIT state allows the client to provide the necessary re-acknowledgment
for the server's FIN, ensuring a clean and graceful shutdown on both ends, =
even
in the face of packet loss.<br />
<br />
</span>&nbsp;</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:14.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold'>Optimized Three-Way Handshake (Less Common for
Termination)</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>In
some cases, if <span style=3D'font-weight:bold;color:#B43512'>the server ha=
s no
more data to send immediately after receiving the client's FIN</span><span
style=3D'color:#B43512'>, </span><span style=3D'font-weight:bold;color:#B43=
512'>it
can combine its ACK to the client's FIN with its own FIN</span>. This would
look like:</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold'><span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0</span>* =
Client sends
FIN.</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold'><span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0</span>* =
Server sends
FIN + ACK (acknowledging client's FIN and sending its own FIN).</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold'><span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0</span>* =
Client sends
final ACK.</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>This
effectively makes it a three-way termination. However, the <span
style=3D'font-weight:bold'>full four-way handshake is the most common and r=
obust
approach.</span><br />
&nbsp;</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:14.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold'>What happens to resources?</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>During
this process, <span style=3D'font-weight:bold;color:#B43512'>the operating =
system
gradually releases the resources associated with the TCP connection</span>,
such as:</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0</span><span style=3D'font-weight:bold'>* =
Socket
buffers</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold'><span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0</span>* =
Port numbers</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold'><span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0</span>* =
Entry in the
connection table</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>This
ensures a clean shutdown and frees up resources for new connections.</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold'><br />
<br />
