<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold'><br />
In summary:</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0</span>* The <span style=3D'font-weight:bo=
ld'>3-way
handshake is for connection establishment, not primary data transfer.</span=
></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0</span>* While a client initiates a connec=
tion and
often an initial request, the <span style=3D'font-weight:bold'>established =
TCP
connection is full-duplex</span>, allowing both client and server to send a=
nd
receive data.</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0</span>* The<span style=3D'font-weight:bol=
d'> server is
indeed responsible for routing its response packets back to the client.</sp=
an><br
/>
<br />
&nbsp;</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:14.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold'>How it is possible to send data simultaneously e=
ither
one of the side will be sender and the other would be acceptor?</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>You've
hit upon a key concept in network communication<span style=3D'font-weight:b=
old;
color:#B43512'>: Full-Duplex communication</span>. But <span style=3D'font-=
weight:
bold'>TCP is designed to enable both sides to send and receive data
simultaneously on the same connection.</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>&nbsp;</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>Let's
break down how this is possible, addressing the idea that one side must be a
sender and the other an acceptor:</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:14.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold'>Two independent data streams for sending and
receiving:</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>Within
a single TCP connection, there will be two independent data streams:</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0</span>* One stream from Client to Server:=
 The <span
style=3D'font-weight:bold'>client has its own sequence numbers for the data=
 it
sends</span>, and the server acknowledges that data.</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0</span>* One stream from Server to Client:=
 The <span
style=3D'font-weight:bold'>server has its own sequence numbers for the data=
 it
sends,</span> and the client acknowledges that data.</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold;color:#0070C0'>Both the client and the server are
continuously acting as both senders and receivers, managing their own outgo=
ing
data streams and acknowledging the incoming data streams from the other par=
ty.
This is the essence of TCP's full-duplex capability.</span><br />
<br />
&nbsp;</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;font-family:Calibri'><span style=3D'font-size:11.0pt=
'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0=C2=A0=C2=A0=C2=A0=C2=A0=C2=A0=C2=A0 </spa=
n></span><span style=3D'font-weight:bold;
font-size:14.0pt'><span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0</span>Buffering a=
t Both
Ends:</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt;
color:#B43512'><span style=3D'font-weight:bold'>Both the client and the ser=
ver
maintain send and receive buffers.</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0</span>* When <span style=3D'font-weight:b=
old'>an
application wants to send data, it writes it to its TCP send buffer. </span=
><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold;color:#0070C0'>TCP then segments this data and se=
nds it
over the network.</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0</span>* When data arrives from the networ=
k, <span
style=3D'font-weight:bold'>TCP places it into its receive buffer</span>. The
application then reads data from this receive buffer.<br />
&nbsp;</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>These
buffers allow for <span style=3D'font-weight:bold'>asynchronous operation.<=
/span>
The application doesn't have to wait for the network to be free to send dat=
a;
it can simply write to its buffer. Similarly, <span style=3D'font-weight:bo=
ld'>incoming
data can be buffered even if the application isn't immediately ready to pro=
cess
it.<br />
<br />
</span><span style=3D'font-weight:bold;color:#C00000'>Synchronous operations
execute sequentially,</span><span style=3D'font-weight:bold;color:black'>
blocking further execution until completion, while </span><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold;color:#C00000'>asynchronous operations run
independently in the background</span><span style=3D'font-weight:bold;color=
:black'>,
allowing other tasks to proceed concurrently.</span><br />
&nbsp;</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:14.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold'>Hardware Support (Full-Duplex Ethernet):</span><=
/p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold;color:#C00000'>At the physical layer, modern Ethe=
rnet
networks </span><span style=3D'color:black'>are predominantly</span><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold;color:#C00000'> full-duplex.</span> This means th=
at <span
style=3D'font-weight:bold;color:#0070C0'>the network interface cards (NICs)=
</span><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold'> at both ends of a connection can truly transmit=
 and
receive </span><span style=3D'font-weight:bold;color:#0070C0'>electrical si=
gnals
on the same cable at the same time.</span> Older technologies like hub-based
Ethernet were half-duplex (meaning only one device could send at a time), b=
ut
switched Ethernet allows for simultaneous transmission and reception.</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><br
/>
&nbsp;</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:14.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold'>Port number:</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri'><span
style=3D'font-size:11.0pt'><span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0</span></=
span><span
