<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold'>Why are port numbers needed?</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>Imagine
<span style=3D'font-weight:bold'>a server that hosts a website, provides em=
ail
services, and also runs a database. </span><span style=3D'font-weight:bold;
color:#C00000'>All these services are on the same server, meaning they share
the same IP address.</span><span style=3D'font-weight:bold;color:#0070C0'> =
When a
packet arrives at that server's IP address,</span><span style=3D'font-weigh=
t:
bold;color:#C00000'> how does the server know if the packet is for the webs=
ite</span><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold;color:#0070C0'>, the email service, or the databa=
se?</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>This
is <span style=3D'font-weight:bold'>where port numbers come in</span>.<span
style=3D'font-weight:bold'> Each service &quot;listens&quot; on a specific =
port
number:</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0</span>* A web server typically listens on=
 port 80
(for HTTP) or 443 (for HTTPS).</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0</span>* An email server might listen on p=
ort 25 (for
SMTP) or 110 (for POP3).</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0</span>* A database server might use its o=
wn specific
port, like 3306 for MySQL.</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>When
a client wants to communicate with a specific service, it sends its request=
 to
the server's IP address and the port number associated with that service. T=
he
server then uses the port number to direct the incoming packet to the corre=
ct
application.</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>&nbsp;</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:14.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold'>Key characteristics of port numbers:</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0</span>* <span style=3D'font-weight:bold;c=
olor:#0070C0'>16-bit
unsigned integers:</span> This means they range from <span style=3D'font-we=
ight:
bold'>0 to 65535. (Port numbers are Host numbers? No, port numbers are not =
host
numbers.)</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0</span>*<span style=3D'font-weight:bold;co=
lor:#0070C0'>
Associated with a transport protocol</span>: Port numbers are<span
style=3D'font-weight:bold'> primarily used by transport layer protocols lik=
e TCP</span>
(Transmission Control Protocol) and UDP (User Datagram Protocol).</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0</span>* <span style=3D'font-weight:bold;c=
olor:#0070C0'>Categories</span>:
Port numbers are broadly categorized:</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0 </span><span style=3D'font-weight:bold;co=
lor:#C00000'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0</span>* Well-Known Ports (0-1023): </span=
><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold;color:black'>These are</span><span style=3D'font-=
weight:
bold;color:#0070C0'> reserved for common services</span><span style=3D'font=
-weight:
bold;color:black'> and are assigned by</span><span style=3D'font-weight:bol=
d;
color:#C00000'> the Internet Assigned Numbers Authority (IANA).</span><br
/>
<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0</span>Examples:</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0=C2=A0=C2=A0=C2=A0 </span>* 20/21: FTP (Fi=
le Transfer Protocol)</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0=C2=A0=C2=A0=C2=A0 </span>* 22: SSH (Secur=
e Shell)</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0=C2=A0=C2=A0=C2=A0 </span>* 23: Telnet</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
