/p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0 </span><span style=3D'font-weight:bold'><=
span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0</span>* Easy scaling:</span> When <span
style=3D'font-weight:bold;color:#C00000'>you spin up new instances, you sim=
ply
associate them with the appropriate pre-defined security groups</span>, rat=
her
than having to manually add rules for each new instance to a single, large
group.</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>&nbsp;</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:14.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold'>Security Group Chaining (Referencing Other Secur=
ity
Groups):</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0=C2=A0 </span>* Many cloud providers <span
style=3D'font-weight:bold;color:#C00000'>allow you to reference other secur=
ity
groups in your rules</span>. This is incredibly powerful. For example, your
&quot;Database Servers&quot; security group can be configured to only accept
traffic from the &quot;Application Servers&quot; security group. This means
that even <span style=3D'font-weight:bold;color:#C00000'>if the IP addresse=
s of
your application servers change, the database security group's rule remains
valid because it's tied to the logical group, not specific IPs. This create=
s a
highly dynamic and secure network.</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt;
color:#C00000'>&nbsp;</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:14.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold'>Troubleshooting:</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0=C2=A0 </span>* When an instance can't con=
nect, <span
style=3D'font-weight:bold;color:#C00000'>having separate security groups ma=
kes it
much easier to pinpoint which specific rule in which group is blocking the
