<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>Example
Scenario for Multiple Security Groups:</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>Consider
a typical 3-tier web application:</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0</span><span style=3D'font-weight:bold'>We=
b Tier:
Instances that serve web content (e.g., Apache, Nginx).</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0=C2=A0 </span>* Security Group: sg-web-pub=
lic</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0=C2=A0=C2=A0=C2=A0 </span>* Inbound: Allow=
 HTTP (80) and HTTPS (443)
from 0.0.0.0/0 (internet).</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0=C2=A0=C2=A0=C2=A0 </span>* Outbound: Allo=
w traffic to
sg-app-servers on specific ports.<br />
&nbsp;</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0</span><span style=3D'font-weight:bold'>Ap=
plication
