<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0</span>* You put the envelope into a mailb=
ag. This <span
style=3D'font-weight:bold;color:#C00000'>mailbag is like the Ethernet or Wi=
-Fi
frame</span>, which gets the letter across the local street or building.</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>&nbsp;</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>Here's
the key: HTTP doesn't physically send data across the network by itself.</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>Let's
break it down in terms of the TCP/IP Model, which is the most widely used
networking model:</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0</span><span style=3D'font-weight:bold'>Ap=
plication
Layer (HTTP lives here):</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0=C2=A0 </span>* This is the layer closest =
to the user.</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0=C2=A0 </span><span style=3D'font-weight:b=
old;color:#C00000'>*
HTTP (Hypertext Transfer Protocol) is a set of rules (a protocol) for how w=
eb
browsers and web servers communicate. When you type </span><a
href=3D"http://www.example.com"><span style=3D'font-weight:bold;color:#C000=
00'>www.example.com</span></a><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold;color:#C00000'> into your browser, your browser c=
reates
an HTTP request (e.g., &quot;GET /index.html HTTP/1.1&quot;).</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0</span><span style=3D'font-weight:bold'><s=
pan
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0 </span>* Other protocols at this layer in=
clude FTP,
SMTP, DNS, etc.<br />
</span>&nbsp;</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt;
color:black'><span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0</span><span style=3D'f=
ont-weight:
bold'>Transport Layer (TCP lives here):</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0=C2=A0 </span>* When your browser<span sty=
le=3D'font-weight:
bold;color:#C00000'> creates that HTTP request</span>, it doesn't just thro=
w it
onto the network. It hands it down to the Transport Layer.</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0=C2=A0 </span>* TCP (Transmission Control =
Protocol) takes
that<span style=3D'font-weight:bold;color:#C00000'> HTTP request data and b=
reaks
it into smaller pieces called segments.</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0=C2=A0 </span>* TCP then <span style=3D'fo=
nt-weight:bold;
color:#C00000'>adds its own TCP header to each segment</span>. This header
contains crucial information for reliable delivery:</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0=C2=A0=C2=A0=C2=A0 </span>* Source and Des=
tination Port Numbers
(e.g., HTTP uses port 80 for the server, and your browser uses a random high
port).</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0=C2=A0=C2=A0=C2=A0 </span>* Sequence Numbe=
rs (to ensure data is
reassembled in the correct order).</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0=C2=A0=C2=A0=C2=A0 </span>* Acknowledgemen=
t Numbers (to confirm
receipt of data).</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0=C2=A0=C2=A0=C2=A0 </span>* Checksums (for=
 error checking).<br />
&nbsp;</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0 </span><span style=3D'font-weight:bold;co=
lor:#375623'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0</span>* TCP's job is to ensure that all t=
hese
segments arrive at the destination, in the correct order, without errors, a=
nd
that the server acknowledges receiving them. If a segment is lost, TCP will
retransmit it.</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0 </span><span style=3D'font-weight:bold;co=
lor:#C00000'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0</span>* This is why HTTP is &quot;under&q=
uot; TCP:
HTTP uses TCP to reliably transport its data from the browser to the web se=
rver
and vice-versa. HTTP specifies what data to send, and TCP specifies how to
reliably get it there.</span><br />
&nbsp;</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0</span><span style=3D'font-weight:bold'>In=
ternet Layer
(IP lives here):</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0=C2=A0 </span>* Once<span style=3D'font-we=
ight:bold;
color:#0070C0'> TCP has segmented the HTTP data and added its header</span>=
, it
hands these <span style=3D'font-weight:bold;color:#C00000'>TCP segments dow=
n to
the Internet Layer.</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0=C2=A0 </span>* <span style=3D'font-weight=
:bold;color:#0070C0'>IP
(Internet Protocol) takes each TCP segment and adds its own IP header</span=
>.
<br />
<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0 </span>This header contains:</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0=C2=A0 </span>* Source and Destination IP =
Addresses (e.g.,
your computer's IP address and the web server's IP address).</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0=C2=A0 </span>*<span style=3D'font-weight:=
bold;color:#C00000'>
IP's job is to route these &quot;packets&quot; (now called IP datagrams) ac=
ross
the internet from one network to another until they reach the destination IP
address.</span> <br />
IP is connectionless and doesn't guarantee delivery, which is why TCP's
reliability features are so important.<br />
&nbsp;</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold'><span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0</span>Ne=
twork Access
Layer (Ethernet/Wi-Fi live here):</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0=C2=A0 </span>* Finally, the IP datagrams =
are passed down
to this layer.</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0=C2=A0 </span>*<span style=3D'font-weight:=
bold;color:#0070C0'>
Protocols like Ethernet or Wi-Fi take the IP datagrams</span>, add their own
headers (creating &quot;frames&quot;), and actually <span style=3D'font-wei=
ght:
bold;color:#C00000'>convert the data into electrical signals or radio waves=
 to
be sent over the physical cable or airwaves to the next device.</span><br />
&nbsp;</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold'>Analogy Recap:</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0</span>* HTTP: The content of your letter =
(the
message you want to send).</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0</span>* TCP: The envelope you put the let=
ter in,
with instructions for reliable delivery (like &quot;confirm receipt,&quot;
&quot;send parts in order&quot;).</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0</span>* IP: The address on the envelope, =
telling the
postal service where to route the letter globally.</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0</span>* Ethernet/Wi-Fi: The mail truck or=
 airplane
that physically carries the letter from one point to another.</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>So,
when you say &quot;HTTP is under TCP,&quot; it means that <span
style=3D'font-weight:bold;color:#C00000'>HTTP is an application-layer proto=
col
that relies on the services of TCP at the transport layer to ensure its dat=
a is
reliably delivered across the network.</span> HTTP defines the language of =
the
web, and TCP provides the reliable delivery service for that language.<br
/>
<br />
&nbsp;</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:14.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold'>TCP is not a protocol like http and https.</span=
></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:14.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold'>Https is a part of TCP protocol..?</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>&nbsp;</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>Your
statements contain some common misconceptions about how these protocols rel=
ate
to each other. Let's clarify:</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0</span>* &quot;TCP is not a protocol like =
http and
https.&quot;</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri'><span
style=3D'font-size:11.0pt'><span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0 </span><=
/span><span
style=3D'font-weight:bold;font-size:14.0pt;color:#00B050'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0</span>This statement is incorrect. TCP (T=
ransmission
Control Protocol) is a protocol. The key difference is that TCP, HTTP, and
HTTPS operate at different layers of the networking model.</span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0=C2=A0 </span>* TCP operates at the Transp=
ort Layer. Its
main job is to provide reliable, ordered, and error-checked delivery of data
streams between applications running on hosts. It ensures that data packets
arrive at their destination correctly and in the right order.</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0=C2=A0 </span>* HTTP (Hypertext Transfer P=
rotocol) and
HTTPS (Hypertext Transfer Protocol Secure) operate at the Application Layer.
These protocols define how web browsers and web servers communicate,
specifically for transferring hypertext (like web pages).</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0=C2=A0 </span>Think of it like this: TCP i=
s the postal
service that ensures your letter gets from one mailbox to another reliably.
HTTP/HTTPS is the language/format of the letter itself (e.g., a formal busi=
ness
letter, a casual note).</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>&nbsp;</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>&nbsp;</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0</span>* &quot;Https is a part of tcp prot=
ocol&quot;</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:14.=
0pt;
color:#00B050'><span style=3D'font-weight:bold'><span style=3D'mso-spacerun=
:yes'>=C2=A0=C2=A0
</span>This statement is also incorrect. HTTPS is not a part of the TCP
protocol. Instead, HTTPS uses TCP as its underlying transport mechanism.</s=
pan></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0=C2=A0 </span>Here's the breakdown of how =
they relate:</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0=C2=A0 </span>* HTTPS is essentially HTTP =
with an added
layer of security: SSL/TLS (Secure Sockets Layer/Transport Layer Security).=
</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0=C2=A0 </span>* SSL/TLS protocols run on t=
op of TCP. They
provide encryption and authentication for the data being transmitted.</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0=C2=A0 </span>* HTTP/HTTPS then run on top=
 of SSL/TLS
(when HTTPS is used) or directly on TCP (for HTTP).</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0=C2=A0 </span>So, the hierarchy is general=
ly:</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0=C2=A0 </span>Application Layer: HTTP/HTTP=
S</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0=C2=A0 </span>Presentation/Session Layer (=
handled by
SSL/TLS): Encryption and security</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0=C2=A0 </span>Transport Layer: TCP (for re=
liable data
transfer)</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0=C2=A0 </span>Network Layer: IP (for addre=
ssing and
routing)</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0=C2=A0 </span>Data Link Layer</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0=C2=A0 </span>Physical Layer</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>In
summary:</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0</span>* TCP is a fundamental transport pr=
otocol that
provides reliability for many application-layer protocols.</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0</span>* HTTP is an application-layer prot=
ocol for
web communication.</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0</span>* HTTPS is the secure version of HT=
TP,
achieved by adding SSL/TLS encryption on top of TCP.</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>They
are all protocols, but they serve different functions and operate at differ=
ent
layers of the network stack, with higher-level protocols building upon the
services provided by lower-level ones.</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;margin-left:.375in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.=
0pt'>&nbsp;</p>

